  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA low pressure system will begin to track through the Upper Great \nLakes today after a warm front lifted north yesterday. This will \nhave some weak shortwave energy to the south of the low center that \nwill traverse across Lake Erie. This will reach down across \nNortheastern Ohio and Northwestern Pennsylvania early this morning \nwith isolated rain showers and possible rumble of thunder or two. \nThis energy will exit to the east quickly and high pressure will \nnose in from the south this afternoon. The surface high will be \nsituated to the south-southeast allowing from sufficient WAA to occur \nacross the region in the form of westerly low level winds. \nTemperatures as a result will rise into the upper 70s to mid 80s \nwith the warmest temperatures across Western Ohio, west of I-71. Out \nof the climate sites, all but Youngstown and Erie, PA are forecast \nto either tie or break the record highs for today. Details on each \nsites record high temperatures can be found in the climate section \nbelow. \n\nLate Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, a secondary cold front \nassociated with the low to the north will sweep north to south \nacross the region. Rain showers and possible thunderstorms are \nexpected to begin in Western Ohio after midnight and increase in \ncoverage into the morning hours tomorrow. The best chance for \nthunderstorms will be across Central Ohio during the afternoon on \nWednesday as the front will be making its way through. Given the \ntimeframe of frontal passage, there will be limited instability so \nthunderstorm chances across Northern Ohio will be lower. \nAdditionally, not expecting more than general thunderstorms at this \ntime, though moderate to heavy rain showers will be possible. \nThe cold front should settle across the Ohio Valley Wednesday \nevening and rain/thunderstorm chances will decrease into \nThursday as high pressure builds in briefly to the north. \nTemperatures will also be muted with the rainfall and highs will\nreach into the upper 60s to low 70s across Northern Ohio and \nincreasing to the upper 70s further south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAfter the brief break in rain, a strong low pressure system is \nexpected to move through the Great Lakes Thursday night into Friday. \nAn upper level trough will begin to deepen across the Eastern \nCanadian Prairies Thursday morning and strengthen as it move \neastward. Models have come into better agreement on the timing of \nthe trough and how it interacts with the cut off low across the \nSouthwestern CONUS. The trough axis should reach the Great Lakes by \nFriday evening and the associated surface low will begin deepening \nrapidly. In addition to the widespread rain showers that began \nThursday evening, as the low deepens and the cold front passes \nthrough, winds will begin increasing out of the northwest with gusts \nup to 40 MPH likely throughout the day on Friday. Behind frontal \npassage, there will be a sharp temperature gradient dropping \ntemperatures down to the coldest of the season so far. Temperatures \nby Saturday morning will be down into the 30s with some areas in \nNorthwestern Pennsylvania dropping below freezing. Sunday morning \nwill expected more widespread temperatures at or below freezing. \nFrost/Freeze headlines are likely for both days.\n\nModel trends have high pressure building into the region quicker \nlate Saturday night with any lingering precipitation clearing out \nearlier in the day. Depending on how quickly the high does build in, \nsome showers across Northwestern Pennsylvania may have a few \nsnowflakes mixed in. With the high building in, skies should clear \nout rather quickly since there will be ample dry air moving in. \nTemperatures will begin to regulate as well being near seasonal for \nSunday and the start of next week.",
